===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|blewe}}, from {{der|en|xno|blew||blue}},<ref>https://www.oed.com/view/Entry/20577</ref> from {{der|en|frm|bleu}}, from {{der|en|fro|bloe|blöe}}, {{m|fro|bleve}}, {{m|fro|blef|t=blue}}, from {{der|en|frk|*blāu|t=blue}} (perhaps through a {{der|en|ML.|blāvus}}, {{m|la|blāvius||blue}}), from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*blēwaz||blue, dark blue}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*bʰlēw-||yellow, blond, grey}}. Cognate with dialectal English {{m|en|blow||blue}}, {{cog|sco|blue}}, {{m|sco|blew||blue}}, {{cog|frr|bla}}, {{m|frr|blö||blue}}, {{cog|stq|blau||blue}}, {{cog|nl|blauw||blue}}, {{cog|de|blau||blue}}, {{cog|da|-}}, {{cog|no|-}} and {{cog|sv|blå||blue}}, {{cog|is|blár||blue}}, {{cog|la|flāvus||yellow}}, {{cog|mga|blá||yellow}}. Doublet of {{l|en|blow|id=blue}}.

Possibly related also to {{m+|en|blee|t=colour}}, from {{cog|ang|blēo|t=colour}}; but direct derivatives of {{inh|en|gem-pro|*blēwaz||blue, dark blue}} in Old English include: {{cog|ang|blāw}} and {{m|ang|blēo|t=blue}}, {{cog|ang|blǣwen|t=bluish, light-blue}}, {{m|ang|blǣhǣwen|t=blue-coloured, bluish, violet or purple colour|lit=blue-hued}}. There seems to be a parallel connection in Germanic between words for ''blue'' and ''colour'', dually exemplified by {{m+|gmw-pro|*blīu|t=colour, blee}} and {{m|gmw-pro|*blāu|t=blue}}; and {{ncog|gem-pro|*hiwją|t=colour, hue}} and {{m|gem-pro|*hēwijaz|t=blue, purple}}.

The sense "obscene, pornographic" is apparently from the colour; various theories exist as to how it arose, including that it is from the colour of the envelopes used to contain missives of the censors and managers to vaudevillian performers on objectionable material from their acts that needed to be excised. {{rfv-etym|en}}

====Adjective====
{{en-adj|er|more}}

# Of a blue hue.
#: {{ux|en|the deep '''blue''' sea}}
# {{lb|en|informal}} [[depressed|Depressed]], [[melancholic]], [[sad]].
#* {{RQ:Chambers Younger Set|IX|passage=“Heavens!” exclaimed Nina, “the '''blue'''-stocking and the fogy!—and yours ''are'' pale '''blue''', Eileen!—you’re about as self-conscious as Drina—slumping there with your hair tumbling ''à la'' Mérode! Oh, it's very picturesque, of course, but a straight spine and good grooming is better.{{nb...}}”}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1904|author=w:Guy Wetmore Carryl|title=The Transgression of Andrew Vane|page=140|publisher=Henry Holt and Company
|passage="Will you play some of the 'Garden' now?" she asked. "I think I should like it. I'm just the least bit '''blue'''."}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1978|author=w:Michael Johnson|title=w:Bluer Than Blue
|passage=''But I'm '''bluer''' than '''blue''' / Sadder than sad.''}}
# Having a bluish or purplish shade of the skin due to a lack of oxygen to the normally deep red blood cells.
#: {{ux|en|My hands were '''blue''' with cold.}}
#: {{ux|en|The divers got them out of the car just in time – they were starting to turn '''blue'''.}}
# [[pale|Pale]], without [[redness]] or [[glare]]; said of a [[flame]].
#: {{ux|en|The candle burns '''blue'''.}}
# {{lb|en|politics}} Supportive of, run by (a member of), pertaining to, or dominated by a political party represented by the colour blue.
## {{lb|en|US politics}} Supportive of, run by (a member of), pertaining to, or dominated by the [[Democratic Party]]. {{defdate|late 20th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|I live in a '''blue''' constituency.&emsp; Congress turned '''blue''' in the mid-term elections.}}
## {{lb|en|Australian politics}} Supportive of or related to the [[Liberal Party]].
##: {{ux|en|Illawarra turns '''blue''' in Liberal washout}}
## {{lb|en|UK politics}} Supportive of or related to the [[Conservative Party]].
# {{lb|en|astronomy}} Of the higher-frequency region of the part of the [[electromagnetic spectrum]] which is relevant in the specific [[observation]].
# {{lb|en|of [[steak]]}} Extra [[rare]]; left very raw and cold.
# {{lb|en|of a [[dog]] or [[cat]]}} Having a [[coat]] of [[fur]] of a [[slaty]] [[gray]] shade.
# {{lb|en|archaic}} Severe or overly strict in morals; gloomy.
#: {{ux|en|'''blue''' and sour religionists;&emsp; '''blue''' laws}}
# {{lb|en|archaic|of women}} [[literary|literary]]; [[bluestockinged]].
#* {{RQ:Thackeray Vanity Fair|chapter=61|passage=Some of the ladies were very '''blue''' and well informed, reading Mrs. Somerville and frequenting the Royal Institution; others were severe and Evangelical, and held by Exeter Hall.}}
# {{lb|en|particle physics}} Having a [[color charge]] of blue.
# {{lb|en|informal}} [[risqué|Risqué]]; [[obscene]]; [[profane]]; [[pornographic]].
#: {{ux|en|His material is too '''blue''' for prime-time}}
#: {{ux|en|The air was '''blue''' with oaths.}}
#: {{ux|en|a '''blue''' movie}}
# {{lb|en|slang|dated}} {{cap|drunk}}.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1847|author=Jacob Carter|title=My Drunken Life, in Fifteen Chapters, from 1825 to 1847|page=76
|passage=My wine I drank and oft got '''blue''' / On brandy, gin and whisky too&mdash; / Until my reputation gay, / Along with care, was cast away &mdash;}}

